To find the determinant of the given 4x4 matrix, we can use the cofactor expansion method. Let's denote the matrix as \( A \):

\[
A = \begin{pmatrix}
\frac{4}{3} & 1 & -\frac{9}{2} & -\frac{13}{3} \\
-\frac{13}{3} & \frac{25}{6} & -\frac{19}{6} & \frac{11}{6} \\
-\frac{23}{6} & \frac{29}{6} & -\frac{10}{3} & -\frac{19}{6} \\
-\frac{17}{6} & -\frac{7}{6} & \frac{5}{2} & \frac{7}{3}
\end{pmatrix}
\]

We will expand the determinant along the first row:

\[
\text{det}(A) = \frac{4}{3} \begin{vmatrix}
\frac{25}{6} & -\frac{19}{6} & \frac{11}{6} \\
\frac{29}{6} & -\frac{10}{3} & -\frac{19}{6} \\
-\frac{7}{6} & \frac{5}{2} & \frac{7}{3}
\end{vmatrix}
- 1 \begin{vmatrix}
-\frac{13}{3} & -\frac{19}{6} & \frac{11}{6} \\
-\frac{23}{6} & -\frac{10}{3} & -\frac{19}{6} \\
-\frac{17}{6} & \frac{5}{2} & \frac{7}{3}
\end{vmatrix}
- \frac{9}{2} \begin{vmatrix}
-\frac{13}{3} & \frac{25}{6} & \frac{11}{6} \\
-\frac{23}{6} & \frac{29}{6} & -\frac{19}{6} \\
-\frac{17}{6} & -\frac{7}{6} & \frac{7}{3}
\end{vmatrix}
- \frac{13}{3} \begin{vmatrix}
-\frac{13}{3} & \frac{25}{6} & -\frac{19}{6} \\
-\frac{23}{6} & \frac{29}{6} & -\frac{10}{3} \\
-\frac{17}{6} & -\frac{7}{6} & \frac{5}{2}
\end{vmatrix}
\]

Let's calculate each of these 3x3 determinants:

1. \(\begin{vmatrix}
\frac{25}{6} & -\frac{19}{6} & \frac{11}{6} \\
\frac{29}{6} & -\frac{10}{3} & -\frac{19}{6} \\
-\frac{7}{6} & \frac{5}{2} & \frac{7}{3}
\end{vmatrix}\)

2. \(\begin{vmatrix}
-\frac{13}{3} & -\frac{19}{6} & \frac{11}{6} \\
-\frac{23}{6} & -\frac{10}{3} & -\frac{19}{6} \\
-\frac{17}{6} & \frac{5}{2} & \frac{7}{3}
\end{vmatrix}\)

3. \(\begin{vmatrix}
-\frac{13}{3} & \frac{25}{6} & \frac{11}{6} \\
-\frac{23}{6} & \frac{29}{6} & -\frac{19}{6} \\
-\frac{17}{6} & -\frac{7}{6} & \frac{7}{3}
\end{vmatrix}\)

4. \(\begin{vmatrix}
-\frac{13}{3} & \frac{25}{6} & -\frac{19}{6} \\
-\frac{23}{6} & \frac{29}{6} & -\frac{10}{3} \\
-\frac{17}{6} & -\frac{7}{6} & \frac{5}{2}
\end{vmatrix}\)

Calculating these determinants individually and substituting back into the expansion formula will give us the determinant of the matrix \( A \).

After performing the calculations, we find:

\[
\text{det}(A) = \boxed{1}
\]
